A||N||5||DisfourDan||Found a Fiero||||1||
Z||000000||DisfourDan||05-28-2001||06:58 PM||||hey guys, jsut got a call back from a guy who owns a Fiero.  Its an 1986 Fiero, didnt state what model, im guessing its a coupe.  Manual trans, i think he said it had a V6.  Air conditioning.  I asked about engine problems, he said there were none, except that he was replacing the fuel injector tomorrow, and it was costing him 140$.  I asked if there was any body damage, he stated there was a bit of damage on the hood (not from accident), and a few other very minor damage in other one or two other places.  The seats were in pretty bad condition.  He said he would sell it to me for 700$.  Also the brakes needed to be replaced (easy as pie).<P>I am gonna go and check out the car sometime this week.  Just wanted some suggestions on what to look for.<P>------------------<BR>Nothing good can come from good.||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroFanaticus2||05-28-2001||07:57 PM||||Make sure the windshield wipers work.||||reg||1
Z||000002||CanadianGT||05-28-2001||08:05 PM||||You might want to check for an exaust leak from the Exaust Manifold.  This occurs quite frequently, and would be easy enough to replace if the darn bolts did not always break off insde the cylinder head.  It was hard to get my car certifed here because of that leak, and I had to pull my head off that is right below the window just to get the Broken bolts out, to fix the leak.  You can do it with it in the car, but its real hard.  Wish I had known how hard before I bought the car!<BR>PeAcE<BR>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000003||RossT||05-28-2001||08:07 PM||||two things to keep in mind:<P>1. Dont't jump on the first fiero you come across.  There are so many out there.  It would be best to look at several before you buy.  If you miss out on a good one, then another good one will come along soon!!  Be patient.  Take pics and post them.  We can help you.<P>2. Always budget some repair money.  That would impress your dad.  Put that money in a savings accounts only take it out for repairs.<P>3. There are several fiero clubs in CA.  You would be very wise to take your dad and talk to some of these people. These people have well maintained cars for sale.  You would be wise to buy from someone that knows fieros.<P>GOOD LUCK!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||05-28-2001||08:33 PM||||If it's a V-6 and an 86. It can only be an SE or a GT. Coupes did not have a V-6 option. My guess is it's an SE. Also that means the trans would be a 4-spd. There were some 86's with a 5-spd/V-6 but those were built near the end of the production run.<BR>Like what was previously stated, look it over very good. There is no need to jump on the first one you find.<BR>Good Luck.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">    <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Urchin||05-28-2001||09:19 PM||||Well considering you are in california, I guess that rules out the typical places to look for rot that us canadians have to deal with  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  In addition to the above, it might be good idea to keep an eye on the front suspension and steering components.  Speaking from experience, they can prove to be costly repairs.<P>------------------<BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_8.jpg"> ||||reg||1
